course: dictionary
lesson: 10450
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many everyday words have more than one meaning. To find the right one, read the context, the words and sentences around it. «An elephant curls its trunk round things to pick them up.» Here the words curls and pick up tell you that trunk is the elephant's long nose.
explain: Now try this: «The children sat under the tree trunk and ate their lunch.» The words tree and sat are your clues. A tree trunk is the thick main stem of a tree, so it cannot be a box or a nose. Ask yourself which meaning fits the words around it.
explain: Then check by swapping your meaning into the sentence. If you read «The elephant curled its box round the grass», it does not make sense, so box is the wrong meaning. When the context is still unclear, use a dictionary, and notice the phrases a word often appears in, such as swimming trunks.
fact: The word trunk has several meanings: a tree's main stem, an elephant's long nose, a large strong box, the space at the back of a car in American English, the main part of your body, and shorts worn for swimming.
fact: «An African elephant has two finger-like tips at the end of its trunk» so it can pick up small things.
fact: «An adult elephant can hold about 8 litres of water in its trunk» and blow it into its mouth, so it does not drink through its trunk like a straw.
fact: «Stay close to the trunk where branches are thickest», because the trunk is the strong main stem that holds the branches up.
fact: «A family car could park across the trunk» of the giant sequoia General Sherman, which shows how wide a tree trunk can be.
exercise: «The children sat under the tree trunk and ate their lunch.» What is a tree trunk? | The space at the back of a car | A large strong box for storing things | The shorts you wear to swim | *The thick main stem that holds up the branches
exercise: «The elephant curled its trunk round the bunch of grass to pull it up.» What is the elephant's trunk? | A large box for its food | The main part of its body | *Its long nose, which it uses like a hand
exercise: «Dad put our bags in the trunk before we drove to the coast.» What does trunk mean here? | Swimming shorts | The thick stem of a tree | A long nose | *The space at the back of a car for bags
test: «Ella pulled on her swimming trunks and ran to the pool.» What does trunks mean? | *Shorts worn to swim | Thick stem | Long nose | Large strong box
test: «The old trunk in the attic was full of treasure.» Which meaning fits best? | *A large strong box for storing things | Long nose of an elephant | Thick stem of a tree | The main part of your body
exam: «Sam's coach said to twist his trunk, not his legs, to keep his balance.» Which meaning of trunk fits? | Thick stem of a tree | Swimming shorts | Long nose | *The main part of the body, without the head, arms and legs
